The following activities are exempt from the special event permit requirement:
(1) Funeral processions;
(2) Activities conducted by the City of Canton or one of its component entities;
(3) Activities involving a demonstration, march, assembly, or other exercise of rights guaranteed by the First Amendment of the United States Constitution ("free speech event") unless the activity will affect the ordinary use of parks, public streets, rights-of-way, sidewalks or the activity will disrupt the flow of traffic on public streets or sidewalks.
(Ord. No. 2017-01, § 1, 1-19-2017)

409:
(a) An application for a special event permit shall be submitted to the city manager or his designee:
(1) Not less than seven days prior to a free speech event;
(2) Not less than two weeks prior to an event if city resources are not required and the anticipated crowd will be less than 100 people;
(3) Not less than 90 days prior to an event if the anticipated crowd will be more than 100 people but less than 500 people;
(4) Not less than 90 days prior to an event if road closures are required for the event;
(5) Not less than six months for any event which is anticipated to have more than 500 people in attendance;
(6) Not more than one year prior to an event.
(b) Contents. The city council shall approve special event policies, including an application form. For every event, there shall be an individual designated as the contact person, who shall be the person responsible for the event.
(Ord. No. 2017-01, § 1, 1-19-2017)

438:
(a) License transfers. No license issued hereunder may be transferred.
(b) Denial, suspension, revocation. Each license granted hereunder shall be subject to denial, suspension or revocation for violation of any rule or regulation of the city now in force or hereafter adopted relating to the operation of the business in question.
(c) Notice of intention. Whenever the city manager determines there is cause to deny, suspend or revoke a license applied for or issued hereunder, the city manager must give the applicant or licensee ten day's written notice of intention to deny, suspend or revoke the license. A hearing will be scheduled wherein the applicant or licensee may present a defense to the suspension or revocation before the mayor and city council or such board as the mayor and city council may designate. The ten-day written notice must include the time, place, and purpose of such hearing, and a statement of the charges upon which such hearing shall be held. After the hearing, the mayor and city council or the board may deny, suspend or revoke the license issued hereunder if any of the grounds set forth in this subsection exist. A license may be denied, suspended or revoked upon one or more of the following grounds:
(1) The applicant or licensee is engaged in the escort and/or dating service under a false or assumed name, or is impersonating another practitioner of a like or different name;
(2) The applicant or licensee is addicted to the habitual use of intoxicating liquors, narcotics, or stimulants to such an extent as to incapacitate such person to the extent that he is unable to perform his duties;
(3) The applicant or licensee is guilty of fraudulent, false, misleading, or deceptive advertising or practices;
(4) The applicant or licensee has been convicted of or has pled guilty or nolo contendere to any sexual offense as set out in O.C.G.A. § 16-6-1 et seq., or to any offense involving the lottery, illegal possession or sale of narcotics or alcoholic beverages or possession or receiving of stolen property, for a period of five years immediately prior to the filing of the application. If after having been granted a license, the licensee pleads guilty or enters a plea of nolo contendere to any of the offenses set forth in this subsection, said license shall be subject to suspension and/or revocation;
(5) The application contains materially false information, or the applicant has deliberately sought to falsify information contained therein;
(6) The applicant/licensee fails to pay all fees, taxes or other charges imposed by the provisions of this article;
(7) The applicant or licensee fails to maintain all of the general qualifications applicable to initial issuance of a license under this article;
(8) The applicant or licensee refuses to accept a client solely on the basis of race, color, national origin, religious belief, or sex. License holders shall not refuse to accept a client unless the client is obviously intoxicated or dangerous;
(9) The establishment is a threat or nuisance to public health, safety or welfare; or
(10) Any other violation of this article.
(d) Process. After the city manager makes a recommendation to the mayor and city council to deny, suspend or revoke a license issued hereunder, the mayor and city council, or such board as they may designate, will conduct a hearing to hear evidence relevant to said decision.
(1) At the hearing, the city manager or his designee proceeds first and presents all evidence and argument in support of the recommendation to suspend or revoke the license issued hereunder.
(2) The mayor and city council members or their designees will have the right to ask questions at any time.
(3) After the city manager makes his presentation, the applicant or licensee or the licensee's legal counsel, will present evidence and argument as to why the license issued hereunder should not be issued, suspended or revoked. The mayor and city council members or their designees will have the right to ask questions at any time.
(4) After hearing all of the evidence and arguments of the parties, the mayor and city council will render a decision. The denial, suspension or revocation of a escort/dating service license is final unless the licensee files a petition for writ of certiorari to the superior court of the county within 30 days of the date of the decision.
(Code 2002, § 54-409; Ord. No. 2007110101, § 54-409, 11-1-2007)

